 <t>A step defined in this document resolves to exactly one of two tokens: <tt>resolved</tt>, when the step's evidence requirements are met, or <tt>unknown</tt>, under the conditions stated below. An implementation MUST emit one of these two tokens for the evidence-set step and MUST NOT emit any other value.</t>
 <t>If the receipt carries an <tt>evidence_set</tt>, a relying party MUST resolve it as follows:</t>
 <ol spacing="compact" type="a">
-<li>Verify <tt>source_count</tt>, <tt>pinned_count</tt>, <tt>fully_pinned</tt>, and set-level <tt>retrieved_at</tt> per <xref target="evidence-set-object"/> (falling back to the derivations there when a count is absent); verify every entry against <xref target="evidence-set-sources"/>, including the possession rule, the entry-distinctness rule, and the <tt>pinned</tt>/<tt>content_kind</tt>/<tt>resource_sha256</tt>/<tt>retrieved_at</tt>-form rules. Any inconsistency is a malformed receipt; gate decision = halt; the implementation reports the specific violated condition named in the rule it failed. When a receipt violates more than one condition, an implementation MUST halt and MAY name any one of the violated conditions, except where this document states a precedence explicitly.</li>
-<li>If <tt>evidence_root</tt> is non-null, recompute it from the <tt>sources</tt> entries per <xref target="evidence-root-construction"/> and compare. A mismatch is a malformed receipt; gate decision = halt; reported condition <tt>root_not_recomputable_from_sources</tt>. A pinned entry whose <tt>snippet_sha256</tt> is absent or <tt>null</tt> MUST be rejected here, before any root computation is attempted --- gate decision = halt; reported condition <tt>snippet_sha256_absent_when_pinned</tt> --- rather than allowed to reach root construction and surface as a downstream error.</li>
+<li>Verify <tt>source_count</tt>, <tt>pinned_count</tt>, <tt>fully_pinned</tt>, and set-level <tt>retrieved_at</tt> per <xref target="evidence-set-object"/> (falling back to the derivations there when a count is absent); verify every entry against <xref target="evidence-set-sources"/>, including the possession rule, the entry-distinctness rule, and the <tt>pinned</tt>/<tt>content_kind</tt>/<tt>resource_sha256</tt>/<tt>retrieved_at</tt>-form rules. Any inconsistency is a malformed receipt; gate decision = halt; the implementation reports the specific violated condition named in the rule it failed. The rules checked here include the NUL exclusion rule and every condition listed in the registry at the end of this section. When a receipt violates more than one condition, an implementation MUST halt and MUST report every violated condition, not only the first it finds; conformance compares the set of reported conditions, never their order. A check that presupposes a member's type is not evaluated where that type check failed: when <tt>sources</tt> is not an array, no per-entry check and no count check against <tt>len(sources)</tt> is evaluated, and when an entry is not an object, no member check for that entry is evaluated.</li>
+<li>If <tt>evidence_root</tt> is non-null, recompute it from the <tt>sources</tt> entries per <xref target="evidence-root-construction"/> and compare. A mismatch is a malformed receipt; gate decision = halt; reported condition <tt>root_not_recomputable_from_sources</tt>. A pinned entry whose <tt>snippet_sha256</tt> is absent or <tt>null</tt> has already halted under (a) --- reported condition <tt>snippet_sha256_absent_when_pinned</tt>, checked per entry whether or not <tt>evidence_root</tt> is <tt>null</tt> --- and so never reaches root construction or surfaces as a downstream error.</li>
 <li>When <tt>fully_pinned</tt> is <tt>false</tt> (declared or derived), this step resolves <tt>unknown</tt>, and the receipt MUST NOT be presented as satisfying offline recomputation. This is a resolution of this step, not a member of the payload.</li>
-<li>If the verifier holds candidate content for a pinned item, compute its SHA-256 and compare with <tt>snippet_sha256</tt>. A mismatch resolves <tt>unknown</tt> for that item and MUST NOT halt. The verifier's report MUST carry a per-item reason for every entry in <tt>sources</tt>, pinned and unpinned alike, distinguishing <tt>content_not_held</tt> from <tt>content_differs</tt>; an unpinned entry's reason is trivially <tt>content_not_held</tt> and MUST NOT be omitted from the report for being trivial. This distinction belongs in the report, never in the <tt>v_verdict</tt> domain: a verifier holding different bytes knows its bytes differ and does not know why.</li>
+<li>If the verifier holds candidate content for a pinned item, compute its SHA-256 and compare with <tt>snippet_sha256</tt>. A mismatch resolves <tt>unknown</tt> for that item and MUST NOT halt. The verifier's report MUST carry a per-item reason for every entry in <tt>sources</tt>, pinned and unpinned alike, naming exactly one of <tt>content_matches</tt> (the verifier holds content whose SHA-256 equals <tt>snippet_sha256</tt>), <tt>content_not_held</tt>, and <tt>content_differs</tt>; an unpinned entry's reason is trivially <tt>content_not_held</tt> and MUST NOT be omitted from the report for being trivial. This distinction belongs in the report, never in the <tt>v_verdict</tt> domain: a verifier holding different bytes knows its bytes differ and does not know why.</li>
 <li>A receipt carrying no <tt>evidence_set</tt> resolves <tt>unknown</tt> for this step and MUST NOT fail it. Receipts issued before this section existed remain valid and continue to verify exactly as before.</li>
-<li>A properly declared partial evidence set is not invalid and the receipt is not malformed. An implementation MUST NOT treat an <tt>unknown</tt> resolution under (c) or (d) as a validity failure or a rejection.</li>
+<li>A properly declared partial evidence set is not invalid and the receipt is not malformed. An implementation MUST NOT treat an <tt>unknown</tt> resolution under (c), (d), (h), or (i) as a validity failure or a rejection.</li>
 <li>A verifier MUST treat an <tt>unknown</tt> resolution as a distinct category and MUST NOT present it as a weaker or partial form of a satisfied offline-recomputation claim. A verifier that reports, displays, summarizes, or forwards an outcome MUST carry the distinction into whatever it emits.</li>
+<li>If <tt>evidence_set_version</tt> is a string naming a version the verifier does not implement, this step resolves <tt>unknown</tt> with reason <tt>evidence_set_version_unsupported</tt>, and evaluation of every version-specific rule stops: the verifier MUST NOT evaluate (a) through (d) or (i), and MUST NOT report any condition those rules define, because their meaning is fixed by the version it does not implement. Only the version-independent structural checks still run: that <tt>evidence_set</tt> is an object (<tt>evidence_set_not_object</tt>) and that <tt>evidence_set_version</tt> is present and a string (<tt>evidence_set_version_absent_or_not_string</tt>). A failure of either is malformed; gate decision = halt. An unsupported version is a limitation of the verifier, not a defect in the receipt. This document defines one version, <tt>ao-evidence-set-v1</tt>.</li>
+<li>This step resolves <tt>resolved</tt> only when all of the following hold: (a) and (b) report no condition; <tt>fully_pinned</tt> is <tt>true</tt>; and the per-item reason under (d) is <tt>content_matches</tt> for every entry in <tt>sources</tt>. Per-item results combine by conjunction: one <tt>content_not_held</tt> or <tt>content_differs</tt> makes the step <tt>unknown</tt>. In every other case that is not malformed, this step resolves <tt>unknown</tt>. A fully pinned receipt whose root recomputes but for which the verifier holds no content therefore resolves <tt>unknown</tt>, meaning not yet recomputed; it still establishes that the issuer signed a commitment to exactly these bytes, which becomes testable when matching content is obtained. As step 7 of <xref target="verification-protocol"/> states, an <tt>unknown</tt> resolution does not halt the gate.</li>
+<li>When conditions of different kinds apply to the same receipt, malformed outranks <tt>unknown</tt>, and <tt>unknown</tt> outranks <tt>resolved</tt>: any malformed condition reported under (a), (b), or the structural checks of (h) makes the receipt malformed, with gate decision halt, whatever else applies; otherwise any <tt>unknown</tt> under (c), (d), (e), (h), or (i) makes the step <tt>unknown</tt>; the step resolves <tt>resolved</tt> only when neither applies. An <tt>unknown</tt> condition MUST NOT mask a malformed condition among the rules this section requires to be evaluated.</li>
+<li>A verifier evaluates this step in this order: (e), and if the receipt carries no <tt>evidence_set</tt> the step resolves <tt>unknown</tt> and evaluation stops; the version-independent structural checks of (h); the version check of (h), and if the version is unsupported the step resolves <tt>unknown</tt> and evaluation stops; (a), reporting every condition; (b), only if (a) reported none; (d) for every entry, only if (b) reported none; then (c) and (i) determine the token, combined under (j). Paragraphs (f) and (g) constrain how any result is treated and presented. The order fixes which rules are evaluated; it never lets an <tt>unknown</tt> reached earlier suppress a malformed condition that this order requires to be evaluated.</li>
 </ol>
-<t>Steps (a) and (b) are checks on the receipt's internal consistency and are answerable offline by any verifier. Step (d) is a check against external content and is answerable only by a verifier that holds it; a verifier that cannot obtain the content has learned nothing about the receipt's validity and MUST NOT report that absence as a defect in the receipt.</t>
+<t>Steps (a), (b), and (h) are checks on the receipt's internal consistency and are answerable offline by any verifier. Step (d) is a check against external content and is answerable only by a verifier that holds it; a verifier that cannot obtain the content has learned nothing about the receipt's validity and MUST NOT report that absence as a defect in the receipt.</t>
